    "prompt": "Function `StreamWriter.write` in aiohttp/http_writer.py. The statements below were written about this exact version of the function.\n\nFunction source (docstring removed):\n```python\nasync def write(\n        self, chunk: bytes, *, drain: bool = True, LIMIT: int = 0x10000\n    ) -> None:\n        if self._on_chunk_sent is not None:\n            await self._on_chunk_sent(chunk)\n\n        if isinstance(chunk, memoryview):\n            if chunk.nbytes != len(chunk):\n                # just reshape it\n                chunk = chunk.cast(\"c\")\n\n        if self._compress is not None:\n            chunk = await self._compress.compress(chunk)\n            if not chunk:\n                return\n\n        if self.length is not None:\n            chunk_len = len(chunk)\n            if self.length >= chunk_len:\n                self.length = self.length - chunk_len\n            else:\n                chunk = chunk[: self.length]\n                self.length = 0\n                if not chunk:\n                    return\n\n        if chunk:\n            if self.chunked:\n                chunk_len_pre = (\"%x\\r\\n\" % len(chunk)).encode(\"ascii\")\n                chunk = chunk_len_pre + chunk + b\"\\r\\n\"\n\n            self._write(chunk)\n\n            if self.buffer_size > LIMIT and drain:\n                self.buffer_size = 0\n                await self.drain()\n```\n\nFacts extracted from this version by a parser (tree-sitter):\nsignature: write(self, chunk, drain, LIMIT) -> None\ncalls: (\"%x\\r\\n\" % len(chunk)).encode, chunk.cast, isinstance, len, self._compress.compress, self._on_chunk_sent, self._write, self.drain\nreturn statements: return | return\nraise statements: (none)\ncontrol flow: if=11 loops=0 try=0 with=0 returns=2 yields=0 awaits=6\n\n\nStatements:\nS1. `drain` is a boolean indicating whether to wait for the buffer to drain before returning (defaulting to True).\nS2. `StreamWriter.write` is an asynchronous method.\nS3. StreamWriter.write writes chunk of data to a stream.\nS4. `chunk` is the byte data to be sent.\nS5. `LIMIT` is an integer representing the buffer size limit (defaulting to 0x10000).\nS6. write_eof() indicates end of stream.\nS7. If the resulting chunk is empty after compression, the function returns early.\nS8. It then processes the chunk, handling memoryview types by reshaping them if necessary.\nS9. The function manages content length constraints, truncating the chunk if necessary and returning early if no data remains to send.\nS10. For chunked transfer encoding, it prepends and appends the appropriate headers and footers around the chunk.\nS11. The function first checks if there's an `_on_chunk_sent` callback and awaits its execution with the chunk as an argument.\nS12. If compression is enabled via `_compress`, it compresses the chunk.\nS13. writer can't be used after write_eof() method being called.\nS14. Finally, it writes the chunk to the stream using `_write` and optionally drains the buffer if its size exceeds the specified limit and `drain` is True.\nS15. `StreamWriter.write` accepts three parameters: `chunk`, `drain`, and `LIMIT`.\nS16. StreamWriter.write() returns drain future.\n\nFor each statement decide:\n- supported: the shown code makes the statement true (a statement about purpose/intent counts as supported unless the code contradicts it).\n- contradicted: the shown code clearly makes the statement false, e.g. a named parameter, call, attribute, return value, exception or condition it describes no longer exists or now behaves differently.\n- unresolved: the statement concerns code that is not shown (callers, helper internals, external systems) and the shown code neither confirms nor refutes it.\nBe strict about \"contradicted\": require concrete evidence in the shown code or the diff; renaming a local variable or reformatting does not contradict a statement about behaviour.\n\nAnswer with exactly one line per statement and nothing else, in this format:\nS1: supp